{
  "summary": "The manuscript reports developing 40 measurable, observable learning outcomes for the GreenComp sustainability competence framework within the OpenPass4Climate project. It uses stakeholder co-design, a two-round expert validation described as a modified Delphi, and a KSA coverage analysis. The authors argue the outcomes operationalize GreenComp for Open Badge recognition, cover all three learning dimensions across all 12 competences, and offer a more transformative alternative to skills-based frameworks. The process is documented clearly and the gaps it addresses are well motivated. However, the validation results are not reported quantitatively, measurement properties are untested (field testing is listed as future work), post-revision coverage is not reported, and several counts are inconsistent. The central claim of validated, assessable outcomes is therefore not yet supported by the evidence presented. The external data repository was not supplied and could not be checked.",
  "issues": [
    {
      "claim": "The two-round expert validation establishes the validity of the 40 outcomes, but the rating results are not reported and the revision rule is not a consensus rule.",
      "issue_type": "V7",
      "status": "missing_information",
      "severity": "major",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "was considered to require revision if any expert marked it as",
          "location_hint": "Methodology, Phase 2: First expert validation round (p010)"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "The expert validation phase employed a modified Delphi",
          "location_hint": "Methodology, Phase 2 (p010)"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "page_images",
          "page": 11,
          "observation": "Phase 4 describes a second round with a 'more focused protocol', but no per-criterion counts, agreement levels, or approval rate for round 2 are given. Only the round-1 split (21 approved, 15 revised) is reported."
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "The validity claim for the 40 outcomes depends on expert ratings, yet no per-criterion results, response rate, agreement statistic, or round-2 approval outcomes are reported. A rule that flags an outcome if any single expert objects on any criterion is not a Delphi consensus criterion, which weakens the label 'modified Delphi'. This affects the conclusion that the outcomes are validated and fit for purpose. The expert ratings and thematic analysis are not supplied in the material, so the limitation is that this cannot be verified from the manuscript alone."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The outcomes are described as observable, measurable and assessable, and as concrete, actionable targets for assessment and recognition.",
      "issue_type": "V1",
      "status": "missing_information",
      "severity": "major",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "formulated to be observable, measurable, and assessable",
          "location_hint": "Abstract, Methods (English abstract)"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "A primary step is to conduct extensive field testing in diverse",
          "location_hint": "Future directions (p016)"
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "Measurability and assessability are asserted by the formulation process and expert judgement, but no assessment criteria, rubrics, inter-rater reliability, or pilot use are reported. The Future directions section concedes that field testing and assessment frameworks are still to be developed. Construct and measurement validity for assessment and badge use is therefore not established. The abstract and Results language overstates what the evidence shows."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The outcomes cover the GreenComp KSAs and address all three learning dimensions across all 12 competences.",
      "issue_type": "V8",
      "status": "missing_information",
      "severity": "major",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "attitudes were less comprehensively addressed (62% coverage)",
          "location_hint": "Phase 3 and Results, Coverage analysis findings (p012)"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "all three dimensions of learning (cognitive, social-emotional,",
          "location_hint": "Discussion, Situating our contribution (p015)"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "page_images",
          "page": 12,
          "observation": "The right column reports attitude coverage at 62%, knowledge 87%, and skills 79%, and Area I gaps (particularly 1.3). The coverage figures are for the initial 36 outcomes only, and no post-revision coverage matrix or figures are shown."
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "The Discussion says the outcomes address all three dimensions across all 12 competences. The only coverage figures given are for the initial 36 outcomes, and attitudes were the least covered. The paper does not report coverage after the four new and three modified outcomes were added, so the claim of complete coverage is not shown. This affects the conclusion that the outcome set is comprehensive and balanced across dimensions."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The expert panel provided independent validation of the outcomes.",
      "issue_type": "V2",
      "status": "suggestion",
      "severity": "minor",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "and authors of GreenComp-related publications",
          "location_hint": "Methodology, Phase 2 (p010)"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "such as TWIN-IN, ASSESS",
          "location_hint": "Methodology, Phase 2 (p010)"
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "The validation panel includes authors of GreenComp-related publications and representatives of projects that the paper itself reviews, including ASSESS, whose co-created criteria the authors describe as in tension with standardized recognition. The co-design group was drawn from the consortium that produced the outcomes. The manuscript does not state whether panel members had prior involvement in the outcome drafts or any conflicts of interest. This matters for the claim that validation was independent and bias was controlled."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Revision counts are reported consistently for the second validation round.",
      "issue_type": "V3",
      "status": "confirmed_error",
      "severity": "minor",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "focused on the eight modified or new learning outcomes",
          "location_hint": "Results, Second iteration results (p012)"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "Three existing learning outcomes were expanded",
          "location_hint": "Results, Enhancement strategies (p012)"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "Four new learning outcomes were developed to",
          "location_hint": "Results, Enhancement strategies (p012)"
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "The text states that three existing outcomes were modified and four new ones were created, which totals seven. The second round is described as covering 'the eight modified or new learning outcomes'. The discrepancy is small but affects traceability of which outcomes were re-validated. The 36 + 4 = 40 total is consistent, so the eighth outcome is unexplained."
    }
  ],
  "overall_assessment": "The paper is well structured and makes a clear, relevant contribution by attempting to convert GreenComp's descriptive KSAs into outcome statements. Its main weakness is that the evidence for the central claim is thin. Expert validation results are summarised rather than reported, the revision rule is not a consensus criterion, and measurement and assessment properties are untested. Coverage claims rest on the pre-revision set of 36 outcomes, and the Discussion overstates completeness. There are also small numerical inconsistencies and an unclear panel independence statement. The outcomes are a useful starting point, but the claims of validity, assessability and complete coverage should be presented as provisional until the expert ratings, post-revision coverage, and pilot assessment data are reported. The external Annex I and expert data could not be inspected, so those judgements are limited to the supplied material."
}
